      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That \"whining\" or \"howling\" sound when the boost hits is often related to the specific aerodynamics and the speed of the airflow, which can be quite complex. When the compressor is spinning at very high RPMs, the turbulent flow of air across the blade edges can generate harmonic overtones that sound less like a simple whistle and more like a sustained whine. The shape of the blades, especially the leading edges, dictates how the air separates and reattaches, creating these distinct tonal qualities.",
